//! The Image module: BMP files, surfaces and textures, on `Window`'s renderer.
//!
//! Written in Jairs. PLAN §8.5's image-decode item, and the last graphics-shaped one — the remaining §8.5 entry
//! is audio, which is not.
//!
//! # Why BMP and not PNG
//!
//! **SDL2 decodes BMP itself.** `SDL_LoadBMP_RW` is in the base library, so this module needs no second
//! dependency on top of the one ADR-0163 §1 already accepted as a stated cost. PNG needs `SDL_image`, which is
//! a separate library with its own version skew — and a decoder written in Jairs needs zlib's inflate, which is
//! a real project and not an item inside a graphics wave.
//!
//! So this is the honest scope: **loading an image works, and the format is the one that costs nothing**. A
//! caller who needs PNG binds `SDL_image` the same way `Window` binds SDL2, and this module is the shape to
//! copy.
//!
//! **Rejected: writing a PNG decoder here.** It would be the largest single thing in the standard library, and
//! it belongs beside a `Compress` module rather than inside `Image`.
//!
//! **Rejected: skipping images entirely** and calling §8.5's item deferred. A texture path that has never
//! carried a real decoded image is a texture path nobody has tested — and the decode is the part with the
//! interesting failure (a missing file, a corrupt header), which is exactly what a caller needs a flag for.
//!
//! # Surface, then texture, and why both exist
//!
//! A **surface** is pixels in ordinary memory. A **texture** is pixels the renderer can draw from, which on an
//! accelerated renderer means pixels on the GPU. Loading produces a surface; drawing needs a texture; so a
//! caller does both and frees the surface. That is SDL's shape and this module does not hide it, because hiding
//! it would mean either keeping every surface alive forever or deciding for the caller when to convert.
//!
//! `load_texture` does the whole sequence for the common case, and `load_bmp` plus `texture_from` are there for a
//! caller who wants the pixels.
//!
//! # Reading a surface's size means overlaying `SDL_Surface`
//!
//! `SDL_Surface` is a struct SDL allocates and hands back a pointer to, and its `w`, `h`, `pitch` and `pixels`
//! are at fixed offsets. So `Surface_Data` is a `#place` overlay (ADR-0144), exactly as `Window.Event` is
//! (ADR-0165 §1) — and for the same reason it is legal: a pointer to it crosses the `#foreign` boundary, and a
//! pointer is just a pointer.
//!
//! **The offsets are ABI, not contract**, and this module says so rather than implying otherwise. `w` at 16 and
//! `h` at 20 are where SDL2 puts them on both supported targets, read from `offsetof` rather than guessed;
//! `layout_is_sdl2` checks the struct's size so a platform that disagrees fails a test instead of reading
//! rubbish. That is a weaker guarantee than `SDL_Event`'s offset 0, which SDL's own header documents — and the
//! difference is worth stating, because a reader who saw both would otherwise assume they were equally solid.
//!
//! # What is absent, and why
//!
//! **No pixel access.** `pixels` is exposed as a `*u8` and deliberately nothing reads it: doing so needs the
//! surface's pixel *format*, which is a second pointer into a second SDL struct, and a caller who wants to poke
//! pixels wants a whole format-conversion API rather than one accessor.
//!
//! **No rotation or flipping.** `SDL_RenderCopyEx` takes an angle and a `SDL_Point` centre **by pointer**, so it
//! would work — but a rotation API wants a decision about the pivot convention, and this module has no caller
//! with an opinion yet.
//!
//! **No sub-rectangle source.** `draw_texture` copies the whole texture. A sprite sheet needs a source rect, which is
//! one more `*Rect` argument and no new mechanism, so it is a small addition rather than a design.

#import "Window";

// `Renderer`, `Rect` and `rect` below come from `Window`, unqualified: an `#import` is flat and there is no
// `Window.Rect` syntax (ADR-0166 §7).
//
// **Which is why every routine here is prefixed** — `fill_surface` and not `fill`, `free_surface` and not
// `free`, `destroy_texture` and not `destroy`. Written unprefixed first, and a file importing `Window`, `Basic`
// and this module got **four E0211 ambiguous-name errors** at once: `fill` and `destroy` collide with `Window`'s,
// `free` with `Basic`'s, and `layout_is_sdl2` with `Window`'s. The compiler catching it is the good outcome. The
// lesson is that in a flat namespace a module must prefix as though the namespace were its own, because there is
// no qualification to fall back on and a short name is a claim on every importer.

/// The SDL2 shared library. Named again here rather than re-exported, because a `#foreign` declaration resolves
/// its library in **its own file** (ADR-0019 §4) and a name imported from `Window` would not be that.
sdl :: #system_library "SDL2";

/// An `SDL_Surface` — pixels in ordinary memory — as a `#place` overlay of the fields worth reading.
///
/// **96 bytes**, which `layout_is_sdl2` checks. SDL allocates it, so this struct is never a local: a caller only
/// ever holds a `*Surface_Data` that SDL wrote.
///
/// The offsets are SDL2's ABI rather than a documented contract — see the module docs, which is where that
/// distinction is argued.
Surface_Data :: struct {
    /// Width in pixels.
    width: s32 #place 16;
    /// Height in pixels.
    height: s32 #place 20;
    /// Bytes per row, which is **not** `width * bytes_per_pixel`: SDL pads rows for alignment.
    ///
    /// Exposed because a caller who ever does read pixels needs it, and because its absence is the single most
    /// common source of a skewed image.
    pitch: s32 #place 24;
    /// The pixels. Nothing here reads them — see the module docs.
    pixels: *u8 #place 32;
    /// The rest of the struct, so the overlay is the 96 bytes SDL allocated.
    ///
    /// Never read. It exists so the size check below has something to check, and so no future field lands
    /// outside the struct.
    tail: [56]u8 #place 40;
}

/// A texture the renderer can draw from.
///
/// A named one-field struct, for `Window`'s reason (ADR-0164 §1): a handle and a count are both machine words.
Texture :: struct {
    /// The `SDL_Texture *`. Null when the texture is not loaded.
    handle: *u8;
}

/// A loaded image's pixels, before it becomes a texture.
Surface :: struct {
    /// The `SDL_Surface *`. Null when the surface is not loaded.
    handle: *Surface_Data;
}

/// `SDL_RWFromFile(const char *file, const char *mode)`.
sdl_rw_from_file :: (path: *u8, mode: *u8) -> *u8 #foreign sdl "SDL_RWFromFile";

/// `SDL_LoadBMP_RW(SDL_RWops *, int freesrc)`.
///
/// `SDL_LoadBMP` is a **macro** in the header, not a symbol, so this is what a `#foreign` declaration can name.
sdl_load_bmp_rw :: (source: *u8, free_source: s64) -> *Surface_Data #foreign sdl "SDL_LoadBMP_RW";

/// `SDL_SaveBMP_RW(SDL_Surface *, SDL_RWops *, int freedst)`.
sdl_save_bmp_rw :: (surface: *Surface_Data, destination: *u8, free_destination: s64) -> s64 #foreign sdl "SDL_SaveBMP_RW";

/// `SDL_CreateRGBSurfaceWithFormat(Uint32 flags, int w, int h, int depth, Uint32 format)`.
sdl_create_surface :: (flags: s64, width: s64, height: s64, depth: s64, format: s64) -> *Surface_Data #foreign sdl "SDL_CreateRGBSurfaceWithFormat";

/// `SDL_FreeSurface`.
sdl_free_surface :: (surface: *Surface_Data) #foreign sdl "SDL_FreeSurface";

/// `SDL_FillRect(SDL_Surface *, const SDL_Rect *, Uint32 color)`.
sdl_fill_surface :: (surface: *Surface_Data, area: *Rect, colour: s64) -> s64 #foreign sdl "SDL_FillRect";

/// `SDL_CreateTextureFromSurface(SDL_Renderer *, SDL_Surface *)`.
sdl_texture_from_surface :: (renderer: *u8, surface: *Surface_Data) -> *u8 #foreign sdl "SDL_CreateTextureFromSurface";

/// `SDL_DestroyTexture`.
sdl_destroy_texture :: (texture: *u8) #foreign sdl "SDL_DestroyTexture";

/// `SDL_QueryTexture(SDL_Texture *, Uint32 *format, int *access, int *w, int *h)`.
sdl_query_texture :: (texture: *u8, format: *u32, access: *s32, width: *s32, height: *s32) -> s64 #foreign sdl "SDL_QueryTexture";

/// `SDL_RenderCopy(SDL_Renderer *, SDL_Texture *, const SDL_Rect *src, const SDL_Rect *dst)`.
sdl_render_copy :: (renderer: *u8, texture: *u8, source: *Rect, destination: *Rect) -> s64 #foreign sdl "SDL_RenderCopy";

/// `SDL_PIXELFORMAT_RGBA8888`, the one format this module creates surfaces in.
///
/// The value rather than the macro, since a `#foreign` declaration cannot name a macro and the header computes
/// this from a bitfield. Checked against the C value rather than transcribed from the formula.
FORMAT_RGBA8888 :: 373694468;

/// Whether `Surface_Data` is the 96 bytes SDL2 allocates.
///
/// A procedure and not a file-scope constant, for the reason ADR-0165 §7 records: `size_of` of an imported
/// struct is not reachable from file scope (E0230), and `Socket` and `Window` both moved the same check.
///
/// **Named `surface_layout_is_sdl2` and not `layout_is_sdl2`**, which is what it was first called: `Window` has
/// a `layout_is_sdl2` too, and a file importing both got **E0211, ambiguous name** — the flat-namespace hazard
/// ADR-0166 §7 recorded, arriving for real one wave later. The compiler catches it, which is the good outcome;
/// the lesson is that a module must prefix the names it exports as though the namespace were its own, because
/// there is no qualification to fall back on.
surface_layout_is_sdl2 :: () -> bool {
    return size_of(Surface_Data) == 96;
}

/// Loads a BMP file into a surface.
///
/// `path` must be NUL-terminated — a `*u8`, not a `string`, for `Window.open`'s reason.
///
/// `#must`: a missing or corrupt file is the *expected* failure of this routine, and it is the reason ADR-0166 §
/// rejected deferring images entirely. A caller who ignores the flag gets a null handle and a null texture and
/// draws nothing, with no clue which of the three steps failed.
load_bmp :: (path: *u8) -> (Surface, bool) #must {
    s: Surface;
    mode := "rb\0";
    source := sdl_rw_from_file(path, mode.data);
    if source == null {
        s.handle = null;
        return s, false;
    }
    // `1` frees the `SDL_RWops` whether the decode succeeds or fails, which is why there is no leak on the
    // failure path below and no second cleanup call anywhere in this module.
    s.handle = sdl_load_bmp_rw(source, 1);
    return s, s.handle != null;
}

/// Creates an empty RGBA surface `width` by `height`.
///
/// Here because the *test* for this module needs one: an image can then be built, saved and loaded back, so
/// nothing binary lives in the repository. A caller building an image procedurally wants it too.
create_surface :: (width: s64, height: s64) -> (Surface, bool) #must {
    s: Surface;
    s.handle = sdl_create_surface(0, width, height, 32, FORMAT_RGBA8888);
    return s, s.handle != null;
}

/// Fills `area` of `surface` with a packed 32-bit colour.
///
/// The colour is packed in the *surface's* format, which for a `create_surface` surface is RGBA8888 — so `0xFF0000FF`
/// is opaque red. A caller mixing this with a surface loaded from a file must know that file's format, which is
/// the honest reason this module has no `set_pixel`.
fill_surface :: (surface: *Surface, area: *Rect, colour: s64) -> bool #must {
    if surface.handle == null {
        return false;
    }
    return sdl_fill_surface(surface.handle, area, colour) == 0;
}

/// Writes `surface` to `path` as a BMP.
///
/// `#must`: a write can fail for every ordinary reason, and a caller who ignores it and then loads the file back
/// gets the *load*'s failure instead, one step removed from the cause.
save_bmp :: (surface: *Surface, path: *u8) -> bool #must {
    if surface.handle == null {
        return false;
    }
    mode := "wb\0";
    destination := sdl_rw_from_file(path, mode.data);
    if destination == null {
        return false;
    }
    return sdl_save_bmp_rw(surface.handle, destination, 1) == 0;
}

/// The surface's width in pixels, or 0 when it is not loaded.
width_of :: (surface: *Surface) -> s64 {
    if surface.handle == null {
        return 0;
    }
    return cast(s64, surface.handle.width);
}

/// The surface's height in pixels, or 0 when it is not loaded.
height_of :: (surface: *Surface) -> s64 {
    if surface.handle == null {
        return 0;
    }
    return cast(s64, surface.handle.height);
}

/// The surface's row stride in bytes, or 0 when it is not loaded.
///
/// **Not** `width * 4`: SDL pads rows, and assuming otherwise is what skews an image.
pitch_of :: (surface: *Surface) -> s64 {
    if surface.handle == null {
        return 0;
    }
    return cast(s64, surface.handle.pitch);
}

/// Frees `surface` and marks it freed. Safe on an already-freed one.
free_surface :: (surface: *Surface) {
    if surface.handle != null {
        sdl_free_surface(surface.handle);
    }
    surface.handle = null;
}

/// Uploads `surface` to a texture the renderer can draw from.
///
/// The surface stays valid and is the caller's to `free_surface`: SDL copies out of it, and freeing it here would make
/// `load_bmp` plus `texture_from` unusable for a caller who wanted both.
texture_from :: (renderer: *Renderer, surface: *Surface) -> (Texture, bool) #must {
    t: Texture;
    if surface.handle == null {
        t.handle = null;
        return t, false;
    }
    t.handle = sdl_texture_from_surface(renderer.handle, surface.handle);
    return t, t.handle != null;
}

/// Loads a BMP straight to a texture, freeing the intermediate surface.
///
/// The common case in one call. It frees the surface **on both paths**, including when the upload fails, which
/// is the leak a caller writing this sequence by hand gets wrong — the failure path is the one nobody runs.
load_texture :: (renderer: *Renderer, path: *u8) -> (Texture, bool) #must {
    t: Texture;
    s, ok := load_bmp(path);
    if !ok {
        t.handle = null;
        return t, false;
    }
    t, uploaded := texture_from(renderer, *s);
    free_surface(*s);
    return t, uploaded;
}

/// Destroys `texture` and marks it destroyed. Safe on an already-destroyed one.
destroy_texture :: (texture: *Texture) {
    if texture.handle != null {
        sdl_destroy_texture(texture.handle);
    }
    texture.handle = null;
}

/// The texture's size, and whether it could be asked.
///
/// Two out-parameters rather than a returned struct, because a struct returned *from* a Jairs procedure is
/// ordinary but the two `s32`s SDL writes are not a Jairs type — the pointers go straight to SDL and nothing is
/// copied.
///
/// `#must`: a destroyed texture reports an error rather than trapping, and a caller who ignores it draws with a
/// zero size, which is invisible.
size_of_texture :: (texture: *Texture, width: *s32, height: *s32) -> bool #must {
    if texture.handle == null {
        return false;
    }
    format: u32;
    access: s32;
    return sdl_query_texture(texture.handle, *format, *access, width, height) == 0;
}

/// Draws the whole of `texture` into `area`.
///
/// Scales to fit `area`, which is SDL's behaviour and the useful default: a caller who wants one-to-one passes
/// the texture's own size, which `size_of_texture` gives them.
///
/// **Rejected: a null `destination` meaning "fill the window"**, which SDL supports. It reads well and it makes
/// the one argument that matters optional, so a caller who forgot it gets a full-window stretch instead of a
/// diagnostic.
draw_texture :: (renderer: *Renderer, texture: *Texture, area: *Rect) -> bool #must {
    if texture.handle == null {
        return false;
    }
    // A null source rect is "the whole texture", which is what this routine means by its name — the asymmetry
    // with `destination` above is deliberate: the source has an obvious whole, the destination has none.
    return sdl_render_copy(renderer.handle, texture.handle, null, area) == 0;
}
